      "documentConcept": "Levodopa and carbidopa formulations and delivery",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:27ddc99a88ef49ed0e1f010d14837d4ea6d4abfd44431a945e619cb2546865e4",
      "text": "Levodopa supplies the dopamine precursor, while carbidopa reduces peripheral conversion and associated nausea. Delivery options include immediate- and controlled-release tablets, extended-release capsules, intestinal carbidopa-levodopa infusion, subcutaneous foscarbidopa/foslevodopa infusion, and intermittent inhaled levodopa. Extended-release oral formulations aim to sustain benefit with fewer dosing occasions, but conversion requires formulation-specific titration. Infusions can provide sustained delivery while adding tube, pump, procedure, or skin-site burdens. Inhaled levodopa addresses OFF episodes alongside existing carbidopa/levodopa treatment. RISE-PD supports IPX203 over immediate-release treatment in patients with fluctuations; it does not establish prevention of future motor complications. Product-specific labels distinguish routes, dose equivalence, and delivery limitations. Regulatory status of subcutaneous ND0612 is time-sensitive: a US complete response letter was announced on October 23, 2025, and EU marketing authorization was announced in April 2026.",
      "sources": [
        "https://crexont.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/CREXONT-Full-Prescribing-Information-August-2024.pdf",
